# Handycam-VR

Extracts clips from a DVD-VR that was recorded from a Sony Handycam, whilst it should work with others this was built
to extract clips from the DCR-DVD203E. The extracted clips will include the recorded date and time extracted from the
`ARI_DATA` in the VOB's, this is then appended to the filename, and included as metadata if an MP4 is produced.

#### Notes

Due to the way the data is positioned over multiple VOB files, the extraction works by creating .VOBs for each clip
using the sector counts. The timestamps to indicate the timed position of each clip (or program) in the .VOBs should be
somewhere (most likely in the IFO), but I was unable to find this in a reliable way so the extraction happens via the
DVD sector sizes. I was also unable to find the format data, it's most likely in the `ARI_DATA` near the recorded date
time, but I couldn't find a link between the data and what the handycam shows, so I've left this out.

## Building

Handycam-VR can be built with or without [FFmpeg](https://www.ffmpeg.org/), without FFmpeg the clips will be extracted
as .VOB files, with FFmpeg these VOB files will be muxed into MP4s with H264 and AAC. To compile with FFmpeg, CMake and
PKGConfig needs to be able to find the FFmpeg libraries from your system.

```
cmake -B build
cmake --build build
```

## Extracting Clips

There's a few ways to run to extract the clips, either give `handycam-vr` the root to the DVD-VR via `-d path/to/dvd-vr`
or specify the path to the `.IFO` and `.VOB` files manually via `--ifo VIDEO_RM.IFO` and `--vob VTS_01_1.VOB`, for
multiple VOBs you can specify the `--vob` parameter multiple times, or pass a comma separated list. For automatic
extraction to work from the root of the DVD-VR, the structure should match the actual DVD-VR (with a
`VIDEO_RM/VIDEO_RM.IFO` and `VIDEO_TS/*.VOB` folders), best to copy these to a local machine instead of reading from the
disc directly.

```
handycam-vr -d path/to/dvd-vr
handycam-vr -i path/to/VIDEO_RM.IFO -b VTS_01_1.VOB -b VTS_01_2.VOB
handycam-vr --ifo path/to/VIDEO_RM.IFO --vob VTS_01_1.VOB --vob VTS_01_2.VOB
handycam-vr --ifo path/to/VIDEO_RM.IFO --vob VTS_01_1.VOB,VTS_01_2.VOB --keep-vobs --video-encoder libx264
```

When FFmpeg is included, the VOB files will transcode into MP4's and the VOB files will be deleted, to keep them
specify `--keep-vobs` as an argument.

Custom prefix for the generated VOB and MP4's can be specified with `--prefix handycam-`, it will default to `handycam-`
when not specified, and the full format of the filenames will be `prefix-clipnum-YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS`. The output directory
will default to the current directory, but can be changed with `--output path/to/output`.

When using FFmpeg, you can customise the video encoder with `--video-encoder`, this defaults to `libx264`, you can also
specify video encoding params with `--video-params` with a default of `preset=slow:crf=22`. This is passed to FFmpeg
via `av_opt_set_from_string` with a key value separator of `=` and pair separator of `:`. Audio encoder can be changed
by using `--audio-encoder`, by default it uses `aac`.

